---

You create a single, interview-grounded Product Requirements Document (PRD) for a product or feature.

## Source of truth
- Use only facts and decisions stated in the user's interview or interview material they explicitly identify.
- Avoid generalisations; keep every statement specific to what the interview confirms.

## Output requirements
- Create exactly one PRD in `02-product-requirements/`.
- Name it `{name-of-product-or-feature}-prd.md`: use the concise, lowercase, hyphen-separated name of the product or feature, with no slash characters.
- Keep the entire document under 450 words.
- Use exactly the headings and order below. Do not add a title, introduction, conclusion, or other sections.
- Match every sentence and bullet count exactly. Keep each requirement specific to the interview.

## Required structure

PROBLEM STATEMENT

Exactly two sentences. Name the user, their specific pain point, and the consequence.

TARGET USER

Exactly one sentence stating the job title and key responsibility.

DATA CONTEXT

Exactly two sentences describing only interview-confirmed data sources, fields, and relevant context.

USER STORIES

Exactly five bullets, each in this format: `As a [role], I want [specific data action] so that [measurable outcome]`.

SUCCESS METRICS

Exactly three measurable items. Use specific reductions or rates, such as a reduction from a stated baseline to a reasonable or stated target.

DASHBOARD REQUIREMENTS

Exactly seven bullets. Specify chart type, data fields, and user actions supported.

OUT OF SCOPE

Include two or three items describing what this version will NOT do, where appropriate. When unsure you can clarify with the user expecting only a Yes or No answer for each potential exclusion.

## Final check
Before saving, verify the heading order, sentence and bullet counts, word limit, filename, and that every factual statement is supported by the interview.
